ce:section.ce:para:
The highest methane production of the two-stage LBR system was obtained during the first two weeks of each section (Fig. 4
                        ) when the highest share of degradable substrate was available. After this period, availability of substrate declined due to discontinuous feeding of the LBR. This is in accordance with Linke et al. (2015) using the same system configuration for analyses of maize silage. According to each section's VFA profile, the highest accumulation of acids appeared as well during the first and second week of experiment (Fig. 5
                        ), which translated into maximum methane production rates. When considering the entire system, methane yields reached values around 300 LN kgVS
                        −1, with maximum yield at the lowest sorghum loading. This result is slightly inferior to previously reported results using maize as feedstock (339 ± 16 LN kgVS
                        −1) in the same reactor configuration (Linke et al., 2015), which is not surprising considering the lower fiber content of maize used in the aforementioned study. Another study reported also higher methane yields of maize compared to sorghum during batch tests experiments (Herrmann et al., 2016). On the contrary, Klimiuk et al. (2010) reported equal methane yield for sorghum and maize with similar fiber content in CSTR experiments. This discrepancy could be explained by the fact that the final methane production is not only related to the crop species, but also depends on the actual fiber content, in which especially lignin has been considered as the most significant influencing variable for specific methane yields (Herrmann et al., 2016).
